                <pb facs="0896" n="1854" sameAs="frus:frus1888p2/pg1854"/>

                <head>No. 34.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Wurts to Mr. Bayard</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">St.
                                Petersburg</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-09-03"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 3, 1887</date>.
                        (Received September 17.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 139.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your instruction No. 99, of the 19th of last month, relating to the measures
                    to be taken for the better protection of the seal fisheries in Behring Sea, and
                    to inform you that, in obedience to it, I have communicated the invitation of
                    the Government of the United States to that of Russia, to enter into such an
                    arrangement as will put a check to the indiscriminate destruction, by the
                    citizens of either country, of the seals in those waters.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">George W. Wurts.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
